mediacodec: a running decoder can be handed another surface

A MediaCodec decoder drew into the surface it was configured with and into
no other: FFmpeg had no call for MediaCodec's setOutputSurface. So whoever
shows the pictures could do nothing with a decoder whose surface was going
away (an Android app's window, each time the app leaves the screen) but
close it, and nothing with one whose pictures had to be drawn instead of
shown as they are but close it and open another. For a player that is the
media opened again: a new connection for a live channel, and a moment with
no picture and no sound.

With this the device a decoder was opened with hands it another surface
while it runs: av_mediacodec_device_set_output_surface(device, surface,
native_window), which is MediaCodec.setOutputSurface (Android API 23)
through Java and AMediaCodec_setOutputSurface through the NDK. It goes
through the device and not through the AVCodecContext because whoever shows
the pictures has the device, which it made, and not the context, which
belongs to whoever decodes; and because it is called on another thread than
the one that decodes. The device has a lock for that: a decoder says when it
has started and when its closing begins (avpriv_mediacodec_device_bind and
_unbind), and between the two the device can move it. A decoder that is
being opened reads the device's surface and takes its own references to it
under the same lock, so the caller may let go of the surface a device had as
soon as the call has returned. Buffers the decoder gave before the call and
that are rendered after it go to the new surface.

The call says what it found: 0, a running decoder was moved; 1, no decoder
has been opened with the device yet, and the next one is configured with the
new surface (a decoder that was reading the old one at that moment is moved
as it is bound); 2, the decoder has been closed; less than 0, the decoder
would not take the surface and draws into the one it had.

The cost: a lock in every MediaCodec device, taken once when a decoder
starts and once when it closes, and a reference to the device held by the
decoder while it is open. Nothing changes for a caller that never moves a
decoder. On a device whose MediaCodec has no setOutputSurface, or refuses
the surface, the call fails and the caller is where it was before.

